from c8e_base import *
LB(8,3,'Language work: synonyms, binomials, participles, gerunds and a descriptive essay','Language work for Spectacular Wonders: synonyms, binomials, participles, gerunds and a descriptive essay','Words for wonderful places',
 "The chapter Spectacular Wonders describes living root bridges, a rare crater lake, glowing seas and a rainbow mountain. Its language work teaches us to say such wonders in richer words, to use fixed word pairs, and to use verbs as adjectives and as nouns.",
 [("Some expressions in the chapter come as a ready-made group, such as came into existence, to name a few, in full glory, in disbelief, to no end and age-old problem. We learn them as whole expressions and use them in our own sentences.","came into existence, to name a few, in full glory, in disbelief, to no end, age-old problem"),
  ("Came into existence means began to exist, as in 'The town came into existence around a river crossing'. In full glory means at its most beautiful, as in 'The orchard stood in full glory in spring'.","came into existence, in full glory"),
  ("To no end means a great deal or without stopping, in disbelief means not able to believe, and to name a few means that only some examples are given from many. An age-old problem is a trouble that has lasted for a very long time.","to no end, in disbelief, to name a few, age-old problem"),
  ("A synonym is a word with nearly the same meaning as another word. The chapter gives us five strong adjectives: enigmatic means mysterious and puzzling, colossal means huge and gigantic, spectacular means stunning and awe-inspiring, serene means calm and peaceful, and exotic means unusual and foreign.","enigmatic, colossal, spectacular, serene, exotic"),
  ("Choosing a stronger synonym makes writing vivid. Instead of 'a big mountain' you can write 'a colossal mountain', and instead of 'a quiet lake' you can write 'a serene lake'. A thesaurus is a book or website that lists synonyms.","synonym, thesaurus"),
  ("A binomial is a pair of fixed words joined by 'and' or 'or', in a set order. The chapter uses hide-and-seek and flora and fauna. The pairs may be synonyms, opposites, rhyming words or words that start with the same sound.","binomial"),
  ("Learn these binomials with their meanings. Odds and ends means small unimportant things. On and off means sometimes. Mix and match means put different things together to get many combinations. All or nothing means something done completely or not at all.","odds and ends, on and off, mix and match, all or nothing"),
  ("Part and parcel means a necessary part of something. Pick and choose means choose only the best. Sooner or later means at some time in the future. Leaps and bounds means increasing or developing very fast. We cannot swap the order of a binomial, so we say 'odds and ends', never 'ends and odds'.","part and parcel, pick and choose, sooner or later, leaps and bounds"),
  ("A participle is a verb form that works like an adjective. The living root bridges, a striking contrast and rugged terrain use living, striking and rugged to describe nouns.","participle"),
  ("A present participle usually ends in -ing and describes an action that is going on, such as a glowing sea or a moving light. A past participle usually ends in -ed, -d, -t, -en or -n and describes an action that is finished, such as carved stone or scattered islands. Irregular verbs may differ.","present participle, past participle"),
  ("A gerund is also an -ing verb form, but it works as a noun. In 'Crossing the waterways was difficult', crossing is a gerund because it is the subject. So the test is simple: if the -ing word describes a noun, it is a participle, and if it names an activity and acts like a noun, it is a gerund.","gerund"),
  ("A gerund can be the subject, as in 'Swimming is fun'. It can be a subject complement, as in 'Her passion is painting'. It can be the object of a verb, as in 'I enjoy reading', and the object of a preposition, as in 'She is interested in learning'. It also follows certain verbs, such as avoid talking.","gerund as subject, complement, object"),
  ("We can join two short sentences with a participle or a gerund. 'Nitesh entered the room. He saw his friend' becomes 'Entering the room, Nitesh saw his friend'. The participle phrase tells us what happened first, and both sentences must share the same doer.","combining sentences with participles"),
  ("For the listening task, listen to the talk on the rainbow mountain with a purpose. Before listening, read the six statements, and while listening, tick only those that match what the speakers actually say. Do not tick a statement just because it sounds likely.","listening for true statements"),
  ("For the speaking task, work in a group of four and present a place of interest in your region over about ten slides. Cover the choice of place, where it is and how to reach it, its attractions and history, weather and food, and the best time to visit.","group presentation"),
  ("For the writing task, write a descriptive essay about something in nature that caught your attention, with a title. Use an introduction of one paragraph, a body of two or three paragraphs and a conclusion of one paragraph, and use vivid words such as serene, colossal and spectacular.","descriptive essay format")],
 ("Underline the participle or gerund in each sentence and say which it is. (a) The glowing waves lit up the shore. (b) Climbing the cliff takes courage.",
  [("(a) 'Glowing' describes the noun 'waves', so it is a present participle.","A participle works as an adjective."),
   ("(b) 'Climbing the cliff' is the subject of the sentence, so 'climbing' is a gerund.","A gerund works as a noun."),
   ("Check each time by asking: does the word describe a noun, or does it name an activity? That decides participle or gerund.","The question gives the answer.")]),
 ("Explain the difference between a participle and a gerund with one example of each, and say what a binomial is.",
  ["a participle works as an adjective, as in glowing waves","a gerund works as a noun, as in Swimming is fun","a binomial is a fixed pair of words joined by and or or, such as odds and ends"],
  ["Thinking every -ing word is a gerund","Changing the order of words in a binomial"]),
 ["Expressions such as in full glory and to name a few are learnt as whole groups.","Synonyms: enigmatic = mysterious, colossal = huge, spectacular = stunning, serene = calm, exotic = unusual.","A binomial is a fixed pair joined by and or or, such as part and parcel.","A participle works as an adjective; a gerund is an -ing word that works as a noun.","A descriptive essay has a title, one introduction paragraph, two or three body paragraphs and a conclusion."],
 [mcq("v3c-1",1,"'Colossal' is closest in meaning to","huge",[("calm","That is serene."),("mysterious","That is enigmatic.")],"Colossal means gigantic."),
  mcq("v3c-2",1,"Which word means 'calm and peaceful'?","serene",[("exotic","Exotic means unusual or foreign."),("spectacular","Spectacular means stunning.")],"Serene means tranquil."),
  mcq("v3c-3",1,"'Enigmatic' means","mysterious and puzzling",[("very old","Not what the word means."),("very loud","Not what the word means.")],"An enigma is a puzzle."),
  mcq("v3c-4",1,"'Part and parcel' means","a necessary part of something",[("sometimes","That is on and off."),("small unimportant things","That is odds and ends.")],"Part and parcel is an essential part."),
  mcq("v3c-5",2,"'Leaps and bounds' is used for growth that is","very fast",[("very slow","The opposite of leaps and bounds."),("complete or nothing","That is all or nothing.")],"Leaps and bounds means fast development."),
  mcq("v3c-6",2,"'Sooner or later' means","at some time in the future",[("sometimes","That is on and off."),("choosing the best","That is pick and choose.")],"It will happen at some future time."),
  mcq("v3c-7",2,"In 'The glowing waves lit up the shore', 'glowing' is a","present participle",[("gerund","A gerund names an activity as a noun."),("past participle","It does not end in -ed or a similar past form.")],"It describes the noun waves."),
  mcq("v3c-8",2,"In 'Swimming is fun', 'swimming' is a","gerund",[("present participle","It does not describe a noun."),("past participle","It is not a finished action used as an adjective.")],"It is the subject, so it works as a noun."),
  mcq("v3c-9",2,"Which word is a past participle used as an adjective?","carved (in 'carved stone')",[("carving (in 'carving is an art')","That is a gerund."),("carves","That is a verb for one doer.")],"Carved describes the noun stone."),
  tf("v3c-10",2,"A binomial can be written with its two words in any order.",False,"A binomial is fixed. We say odds and ends, not ends and odds."),
  mcq("v3c-11",3,"Combine with a participle: 'Smitha walked carelessly. She slipped on the mat.'","Walking carelessly, Smitha slipped on the mat.",[("Walked carelessly, Smitha slipped on the mat.","The participle must be -ing here because the action was going on."),("Walk carelessly, Smitha slipped.","This is not a participle.")],"Walking carelessly shows what she was doing when she slipped."),
  mcq("v3c-12",3,"Which structure is right for a descriptive essay?","title, one introduction, two or three body paragraphs, one conclusion",[("only a title and a long paragraph","The structure needs separate parts."),("conclusion first, then title","The order is wrong.")],"A descriptive essay has a title and a clear order.")])
